#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192587 :question/id #uuid "f20f46d9-b799-57a2-90bc-12e6593e8655" :question/math-academy-id "e-13999" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y=8 \\\\ x-2y=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n![](images/a7/a7e5f9f09d67dd14a4797e5675585973e3aafeb1ee96e9021d245f49ce9c1c7e.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2x}+3y & =8 \\\\ \\cancel{-2x}+4y & =6 \\\\ (3y+4y) & =(8+6) \\\\ 7y & =14 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 2.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y = 2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =-3 \\\\ x-2(2) & =-3 \\\\ x-4 & =-3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1, \\,y=2,$ which can be expressed as $(1,2).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbf4e1a-2c87-562d-8c4d-41fd32e79b86" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9dc4fdb-fc7f-57b9-bbcb-171440bb6b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191189} {:answer/id #uuid "7891246f-146f-5da4-be15-343b7425f61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508564} {:answer/id #uuid "8468d23b-6f42-50bf-9e79-d910d35e0ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508587} {:answer/id #uuid "ac47150e-5e39-5c69-9314-ddb4609e8210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508651} {:answer/id #uuid "f55c1c33-5ff5-5f10-90f5-08f56a27906c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9dc4fdb-fc7f-57b9-bbcb-171440bb6b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191189} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d02a81e-fa71-57d8-a298-b58e206ffcea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c0138a9-c275-57a0-80c9-430f32b2f421" :question/math-academy-id "q-18787" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-5y=0 \\\\ 2x-3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $x.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $x$-coefficient in the first equation is $4,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-5y=0 \\\\ (\\,2x-3y=2\\,)\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-5y=0 \\\\ -4x+6y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{4x}-5y & =0 \\\\ -\\cancel{4x}+6y & =-4 \\\\ (-5y+6y) & =(0-4) \\\\ y & =-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=-4.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=-4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}4x-5y & =0 \\\\ 4x-5(-4) & =0 \\\\ 4x+20 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-20 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-5,\\,y=-4,$ which can be expressed as $(-5,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "1baa2398-4954-5c79-9541-1319e47e02cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191226} {:answer-field/id #uuid "652cda5c-8a31-5ce3-bccf-786c2da038cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191227}] :question/id #uuid "96c8f94a-3d88-537c-8d5d-fa47a28c510f" :question/math-academy-id "q-208816" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n${4x + 3y = 5; - x + 7y =-9$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $x$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dddb3dd-6633-5329-a169-4b39e35735ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dddb3dd-6633-5329-a169-4b39e35735ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508628} :answer-field/id #uuid "2696769d-e344-58f2-b122-e8524fb62262" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508826} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5032cf38-f7e1-5f19-b56e-8d84bbbcec8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5032cf38-f7e1-5f19-b56e-8d84bbbcec8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508509} :answer-field/id #uuid "2adc4195-f1d2-500e-8a09-82e4a8b25e53" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4775db8b-54b5-507a-83b4-6fee15253323" :question/math-academy-id "q-238082" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ 2x+6y=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $(x,y)$ to this system is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the  first  equation is $6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ (\\,2x+6y=12\\,)\\,\\times (-3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ -6x-18y=-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{6x}+13y & =21 \\\\ -\\cancel{6x}-18y & =-36 \\\\ (13y-18y) & =(21-36) \\\\ -5y & =-15 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=3.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+13y & =21 \\\\ 6x+13(3) & =21 \\\\ 6x+39 & =21 \\\\ 6x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,3).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable"}]}
